<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>International news from the Americas - France 24</title><subtitle type="html">FRANCE 24’s coverage of the political, social and economic issues in the United States, Canada, Mexico, Argentina, Brazil and across the Americas.</subtitle><id>https://www.france24.com/en/americas/rss</id><updated>2026-11-04T16:25:55Z</updated><author><name>rssync</name></author><link rel="self" href="rss" type="application/atom+xml" /><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/americas/" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/americas/rss" type="application/rss+xml" /><entry><title>US Midterms: How will Trump manage working with Democrats?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:9ba71548039a32759daf13583c707cb92edfef8d8d65be66f7444893741d4489</id><updated>2026-11-04T16:25:55Z</updated><published>2026-11-04T16:24:56Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/us-midterms-how-will-trump-manage-working-with-democrats" /><summary type="html">Democrats are riding their sweep of Congress into a new era, eager to meet voter demands for change with plans to tackle high costs of living. How will Donald Trump manage to work with them? FRANCE 24's Ophelie Jacobson has more.</summary></entry><entry><title>Tale of 2 midterms: Republican redistricting 'backfired' while Democrats 'exceeded all expectations'</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:f8870578474599bbbc2f6737c7f26dac357cac76b8fa3579428d76078c12e6d3</id><updated>2026-11-04T16:25:55Z</updated><published>2026-11-04T16:09:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tale-of-2-midterms-republican-redistricting-backfired-while-democrats-exceeded-all-expectations" /><summary type="html">Following a resounding Midterm election defeat, Is MAGA Losing Its Grip on the Republican Party? Erin Ogunkeye is pleased to welcome Bryan Lanza, Republican strategist and Partner in Mercury's Washington DC office. "The big thing about MAGA was confrontation, in your face," explained Lanza. But following a veritable blue wave, he believes the electorate has delivered a warning: "We need to move beyond that." While Donald Trump has acknowledged the Democratic victory, at times combative, at times conciliatory, Lanza argues that the president must begin his final two years in office reversing damaging tariffs, addressing widespread dissatisfaction with immigration enforcement and navigating a highly unpopular Iran war.</summary></entry><entry><title>Trump after the midterms: Gridlock, impeachment and succession?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:f3110d9bbc71e8d101b687d2c2dc69dc6ae7c23c13c68a896ffe9cda770bbb30</id><updated>2026-11-04T16:25:55Z</updated><published>2026-11-04T15:54:58Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261104-trump-after-the-midterms-gridlock-impeachment-and-succession" /><summary type="html">Following the US midterms, US President Donald Trump could face a turbulent final two years in office. From budget battles to potential impeachment proceedings, what does the new political balance of power mean for his presidency? FRANCE 24's International Affairs Editor Philip Turle breaks it down.</summary></entry><entry><title>US midterm elections: 'Maybe the best colour for Texas is purple'</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:02e0326dd729be2e43c99fe4d2bc5f6c57b0127a4b7b03d1c9575672b3af33eb</id><updated>2026-11-04T16:25:55Z</updated><published>2026-11-04T15:45:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/us-midterm-elections-maybe-the-best-colour-for-texas-is-purple" /><summary type="html">Texas flipped blue following the US midterm elections, marking a historic shift for a state that hasn't been led by Democrats in decades. The legacy of prolonged Republican leadership has left a lasting mark, but as Democrat James Talarico said with this election, 'maybe the best colour for Texas is purple.' FRANCE 24's Texas correspondent Andres Espinal reports.</summary></entry><entry><title>'Is Republican Party able to pivot away from MAGA movement, or will it sputter forward leaderless?'</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:c18897ee2a2d4e6d27dd0f4483c762a2c499ef63f6ad59c26493704d0724ca91</id><updated>2026-11-04T16:25:55Z</updated><published>2026-11-04T15:16:13Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/is-republican-party-able-to-pivot-away-from-maga-movement-or-will-it-sputter-forward-leaderless" /><summary type="html">Does an electoral repudiation of Donald Trump also mark the beginning of MAGA’s decline? Delano D'Souza is pleased to welcome Ian Reifowitz, SUNY Distinguished Professor of Historical Studies at Empire State University, who describes the Republican defeat as “an absolute and complete repudiation” of the president. He connects voters’ economic frustrations with resentment over presidential enrichment, while questioning whether ideological differences between progressive and moderate Democrats proved decisive. Looking towards 2028, Reifowitz identifies a central Republican dilemma: “If JD Vance is the nominee, he can’t run against Donald Trump”, yet Trump himself may refuse to relinquish control of the party.</summary></entry><entry><title>US midterm elections: Gen Z gives Democrats their strongest support</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:1e51c191fe8f9cba6e4adc4190e69d20f47ff1a820cbfb65cb922a986645a08e</id><updated>2026-11-04T16:25:55Z</updated><published>2026-11-04T15:10:13Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261104-us-midterm-elections-gen-z-gives-democrats-their-strongest-support" /><summary type="html">In the US midterm elections, 50 million members of Gen Z were eligible to vote. Young voters gave Democrats the strongest support, with 75% of them voting for the party. This generation will be the biggest voting bloc in the United States in the coming years. FRANCE 24's Ophelie Jacobson has more.</summary></entry><entry><title>Midterms: How Abdul El-Sayed used 'humour and hope' to rebuff racism</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:6b7e994115daef6ddec3602fa9f998aff03cf62551c1e8b50325497cc99d819f</id><updated>2026-11-04T16:25:55Z</updated><published>2026-11-04T14:25:15Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261104-midterms-how-abdul-el-sayed-used-humour-and-hope-to-rebuff-racism" /><summary type="html">Abdul El-Sayed is set to become the first Muslim senator in US history following his midterms victory in Michigan. His victory came despite what he has denounced as racist attacks against him, inlcuding from Donald Trump, which, says FRANCE 24's correspondent Jessica Le Masurier, he rebuffed with a mixture of "humour and hope".</summary></entry><entry><title>US Midterms: 'It is now going to be pretty chaotic for Donald Trump'</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:e5a088a7c4635a64caf908b343a6f6f3343b8e4178586f40e168dac8e52621b1</id><updated>2026-11-04T16:25:55Z</updated><published>2026-11-04T14:12:15Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261104-us-midterms-it-is-now-going-to-be-pretty-chaotic-for-donald-trump" /><summary type="html">The Democrats' victory in the midterm elections have left Donald Trump in a difficult position for the rest of his mandate. The now "blue" House of Representatives will decide the budget, which Trump will have to accept and follow. FRANCE 24's international affairs editor Philip Turle has more.</summary></entry><entry><title>Ukrainian's 'not expecting drastic change in US policy' after midterms</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:faaeab17593490281f8756f044aca71c689b24c775d883d7acd38b9bbe779c59</id><updated>2026-11-04T16:25:55Z</updated><published>2026-11-04T13:26:11Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/ukrainian-s-not-expecting-drastic-change-in-us-policy-after-midterms" /><summary type="html">Ukrainians do not expect the Democrats' victory in the US midterm elections is change much in regards to US policy on the war with Russia, or at least not quickly, says France 24's correspondent in Kyiv Emmanuelle Chaze. Ukraine's President Volodymyr Zelensky has not reacted yet to the "blue wave" taking control of Congress in the US.</summary></entry><entry><title>A look back at how Trump's political decisions saw his approval rating plummet</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:d0f38132caa963fb79fc271bee69a9eaba51850b4f72b218cd10f239ed529ffc</id><updated>2026-11-04T16:25:55Z</updated><published>2026-11-04T13:17:49Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261104-a-look-back-at-how-trump-s-political-decisions-saw-his-approval-rating-plummet" /><summary type="html">Donald Trump's unpopularity has been cited as a major factor in the Democrats' sweeping victory in the midterms. The latest polls show Trump's approval rating has plummeted to 37.3%, down from 42% in his first midterm election. What were the political decisions that led to such a dramatic decline?</summary></entry><entry><title>Blue wave sweeps across America: 'In 1974, we had Watergate babies, now we have Iran War babies'</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:4508f036237561b4b8fc6ba4dc619fb39feee6d506911b59f901f4d3812ddeb2</id><updated>2026-11-04T16:25:55Z</updated><published>2026-11-04T13:00:59Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/blue-wave-sweeps-across-america-in-1974-we-had-watergate-babies-now-we-have-iran-war-babies" /><summary type="html">Stuart Norval welcomes France 24 International Affairs Editor Philip Turle and Hind Ziane, Geopolitical analyst and founder / CEO of Genpo. "In 1974, we had the Watergate babies. Now we have the Iran War babies," remarks Ziane with this striking historical analogy. Just as the Watergate scandal helped propel a new generation of Democratic lawmakers into Congress in 1974, Zian argues that public disillusionment with the Iran war, mounting economic pressures and dissatisfaction with Donald Trump's leadership may now be reshaping the American political landscape. Yet Turle reminds us, "They're not voting for Democrats because they love the Democrats. They're voting for the Democrats because they don't like Donald Trump."</summary></entry><entry><title>What consequences for Israel as Democrats win the US midterm elections?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:f1f4c3cfa964b18569be0d3a046d1cb4c39644b7f326f67ea484c1ddbc2184d8</id><updated>2026-11-04T16:25:55Z</updated><published>2026-11-04T11:32:43Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261104-what-consequences-for-israel-as-democrats-win-the-us-midterm-elections" /><summary type="html">Democrats seized control of the US Congress on Tuesday, capturing the Senate and House of Representatives in a stunning upset. What are the potential consequences for Israel and Benjamin Netanyahu, as Trump announced on Tuesday that the US will halt $3.8 billion in aid to the country? FRANCE 24's correspondent Noga Tarnopolsky has more.</summary></entry><entry><title>US midterms: Trump reacts to the Democrats’ sweeping victory</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:9fe4439729c4b1f7145ec123b930bb54650d6df67312150ee120f400c133d64c</id><updated>2026-11-04T16:25:55Z</updated><published>2026-11-04T11:24:24Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261104-us-midterms-trump-reacts-to-the-democrats-sweeping-victory" /><summary type="html">US President Donald Trump had rare praise for rival Democrats Tuesday after their stunning US midterm elections victory and admitted the Iran war may have played a part in the defeat of his Republican Party. But the 80-year-old refused to take any personal responsibility for the result.</summary></entry><entry><title>US midterms bring surge of historic firsts for women and minorities</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:226462df6cb78620119f4b8b6243664e24efe7cd9ff6bf2fb5b2ddedfb46927c</id><updated>2026-11-04T16:25:55Z</updated><published>2026-11-04T11:15:39Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261104-us-midterms-bring-surge-of-historic-firsts-women-members-minority-groups" /><summary type="html">The 2026 US midterm elections will go down in history for the Democrats' dramatic takeover of both houses of Congress but also for a surge of historic firsts for women, LGBT, Black and Native American candidates, as well as the election of the first Muslim US senator.</summary><category term="Americas" /></entry><entry><title>US voters back abortion rights and reject Trump's gerrymandering in state ballot measures</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:061d9fc7326e4d5477a2d44ec07f87e9e1f9b750bf30d79dc8827d55a00e97cd</id><updated>2026-11-04T16:25:55Z</updated><published>2026-11-04T10:49:40Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261104-us-voters-back-abortion-rights-and-reject-trump-s-gerrymandering-in-state-ballot-measures" /><summary type="html">US voters in several states pushed back on President Donald Trump’s redistricting efforts and backed abortion rights in 145 ballot questions during the midterm elections. Some measures put Trump’s priorities to a public test by gauging support for his attempts to redraw congressional districts, limit reproductive rights and strengthen voter identification requirements.</summary><category term="Americas" /></entry><entry><title>'Blue tsunami' in US midterms 'pummels GOP out of power'</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:626cc46c20c6388437a4c90b28a3ddcca7850c7ff35cc382ac422a6d972ef59a</id><updated>2026-11-04T16:25:55Z</updated><published>2026-11-04T09:53:27Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/press-review/20261104-blue-tsunami-in-us-midterms-pummels-gop-out-of-power" /><summary type="html">PRESS REVIEW – Wednesday, November 4: US papers react to the results of the midterm elections, with Democrats retaking control of Congress and the headlines running from a "repudiation" of US President Donald Trump to a "blue tsunami". We also look at the historic firsts celebrated by the local press, the blame game already under way, and what the results mean for Trump's final two years in office.</summary><category term="Press Review" /></entry><entry><title>Why the Democrats routed the Republicans in the US midterm elections</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:c795ef5a35d499d25f43c9e1d324e7d6188136f6a4e84d76ff103359fef77863</id><updated>2026-11-04T16:25:55Z</updated><published>2026-11-04T09:41:10Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261104-why-the-democrats-routed-republicans-us-midterm-elections" /><summary type="html">US voters on Tuesday handed the Democratic Party control over Congress in a biting rebuke to President Donald Trump and his MAGA movement. Polls suggest that rising anger about the cost-of-living crisis partly set off by the president’s decision to launch the US-Israeli war on Iran played a crucial part in the Democrats’ historic victory.</summary><category term="Americas" /></entry><entry><title>Democrat Abdul El-Sayed to become first Muslim US senator after winning Michigan</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:00ba91c5c01f84b44d0290acdd6f304d91a1296deb6c57b68bfa5afaab827d6f</id><updated>2026-11-04T16:25:55Z</updated><published>2026-11-04T09:20:05Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261104-democrat-abdul-el-sayed-to-become-first-muslim-senator-after-winning-michigan-race" /><summary type="html">Democrat Abdul El-Sayed has defeated Republican Mike Rogers in a bitter campaign for Michigan’s open Senate seat, making him the first Muslim elected to the upper chamber of the US Congress. During his campaign, El-Sayed had to contend with Islamophobic attacks from Republicans and his Democratic primary opponent endorsing Rogers.</summary><category term="Americas" /></entry><entry><title>Trump congratulates Democrats on midterms win, says Iran war played role</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:02e32b11035379e7272e7775c0e09421807f24b9e69e02eb19ed6d532170411d</id><updated>2026-11-04T16:25:55Z</updated><published>2026-11-04T07:13:41Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261104-trump-congratulates-democrats-on-midterms-win-says-iran-war-played-role" /><summary type="html">US President Donald Trump said rival Democrats had done "a good job" Tuesday after they gained control of Congress in the midterm elections. While Trump acknowledged that the Iran war may have played a part in the defeat of his Republican Party, he did not take personal responsability for the result that will upend the last two years of his presidency.</summary><category term="Americas" /></entry><entry><title>Key Senate results: Democrats retake majority with surprise victories in Texas, Iowa and beyond</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:12d26d8a948499082791f5a882651f65a79a461ff896422cae37b88e418ce964</id><updated>2026-11-04T16:25:55Z</updated><published>2026-11-04T03:52:19Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261104-key-us-senate-results-democrats-wrest-north-and-south-carolina-from-the-republicans" /><summary type="html">Democrats won control of the Senate in the US midterm elections on Tuesday, with the party securing surprise victories in reliably red states including Texas, Kansas, Iowa, and North and South Carolina.</summary><category term="Americas" /></entry><entry><title>Trump fatigue, US democracy, cost of living are top issues with voters, AP survey finds</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:3584eaf514a4c258a8a64d153e31b1e5122a1ada3ed80f3c1f4f95ebb4757539</id><updated>2026-11-04T16:25:55Z</updated><published>2026-11-03T23:46:02Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261103-trump-fatigue-cost-of-living-and-corruption-are-top-issues-with-us-voters-ap-survey-finds" /><summary type="html">Roughly 6 in 10 voters deeply disapprove of US President Donald Trump's presidency, according to an AP survey released Tuesday. About 4 in 10 say the economy is the most important issue while 3 in 10 say the state of US democracy is of primary concern – well above the number that rated crime or immigration as most important.</summary><category term="Americas" /></entry><entry><title>Historian Timothy Snyder: Trump running US foreign policy 'as though he were an organised criminal'</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:cbbeef19747687e61029139052e021d0ce72e00e0c40f186a546e544d5b750b6</id><updated>2026-11-04T16:25:55Z</updated><published>2026-11-03T22:28:56Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/spotlight/20261103-historian-timothy-snyder-trump-running-us-foreign-policy-as-though-he-were-an-organized-criminal" /><summary type="html">As Americans head to the polls for the midterm elections, François Picard welcomes historian Timothy Snyder, professor at the University of Toronto's Munk School of Global Affairs, for a conversation about the future of American democracy, the growing political influence of technology billionaires and the dangers of a new Gilded Age. Who really holds power in Donald Trump's America: the president or the billionaires surrounding him?</summary><category term="Spotlight" /></entry><entry><title>Trump promised a 'Golden Age'. What has he delivered at home?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:c00869811749fbc5fa30a8c105c7bf8f8d9f813e3030f3d3f69ed8621c5924bf</id><updated>2026-11-04T16:25:55Z</updated><published>2026-11-03T20:56:49Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/truth-or-fake/20261103-trump-promised-a-golden-age-what-has-he-delivered-at-home" /><summary type="html">Donald Trump promised Americans a "Golden Age" when he returned to the White House in January 2025. Since then, his administration has slashed government spending through DOGE, ramped up mass deportations, expanded ICE operations and pursued an aggressive crackdown on DEI programmes and transgender rights. But how much has he actually achieved, and at what cost? Vedika Bahl examines Trump's domestic agenda since returning to office.</summary><category term="Truth or Fake" /></entry><entry><title>Trickle or tsunami? Democrats eye gains in US midterm elections</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:4a1ded5e3b153e25252c6cc7d2d282b180ff0881c09962877ae3ce2b6e78da6a</id><updated>2026-11-04T16:25:55Z</updated><published>2026-11-03T19:18:13Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/the-debate/20261103-trickle-or-tsunami-democrats-eye-gains-in-us-midterm-elections" /><summary type="html">Will it be a trickle, a wave, or a tsunami? It's Election Day in the United States, with Democrats hoping to take back one or both houses of Congress. We track the turnout, gauge the mood and raise the curtain ahead of midterm results that are sure to shape the last two years of Donald Trump's second and final term. Will a US president who’s already broken norms with his maximum use of executive powers be affected by the results a little, a lot, or not at all?</summary><category term="The Debate" /></entry><entry><title>Family wealth, Epstein, ‘vanity projects’: How Democrats plan to investigate Trump after midterms</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:92f1f01c96ea9592f8cf3f631f516b5def950d88e51405f0d361d4bf26e692f1</id><updated>2026-11-04T16:25:55Z</updated><published>2026-11-03T17:30:16Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261103-trump-family-wealth-epstein-vanity-projects-democrats-plan-to-investigate-after-midterms" /><summary type="html">Democrats are preparing to launch a flurry of investigations into President Donald Trump’s administration and business dealings after flipping both chambers of Congress in Tuesday's midterm elections, devising strategies to circumvent obstruction from the White House and avoid the impeachment deadlock that consumed the US legislature during Trump’s first term in office.</summary><category term="Americas" /></entry><entry><title>US Midterms: Texas Democrat Talarico holds last rally ahead of vote</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:3ced9f0be510b4ae82ee20ecd962e620a3e7010560a43fdedc667715c66c0e6c</id><updated>2026-11-04T16:25:55Z</updated><published>2026-11-03T16:50:55Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261103-us-midterms-texas-democrat-talarico-holds-last-rally-ahead-of-vote" /><summary type="html">Texas Democratic nominee James Talarico held a final rally Monday ahead of US midterm elections on November 3. His race against Republican Ken Paxton could determine which party holds a majority in the Senate. If Talarico wins, it will be the first time a Texas Senate seat's turned blue since 1988.</summary></entry><entry><title>US midterm elections: Over 58 million Americans cast early ballots</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:b05f9e07386ab17e97c36b118d7cc15cece4360d59c63e17f8b8a2ad246db798</id><updated>2026-11-04T16:25:55Z</updated><published>2026-11-03T16:46:29Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261103-us-midterm-elections-over-58-million-americans-cast-early-ballots" /><summary type="html">How powerful is US President Donald Trump? That’s the question pollsters have been asking in the lead-up to the midterm elections. Polls have opened in dozens of US states in what is widely considered a referendum on Donald Trump. Most polls suggest Democrats will take back the House of Representatives, but could they also take the Senate and stymie Trump’s agenda during his final two years in office?</summary></entry><entry><title>US midterms: Talarico seeks to break Democrats' 30-year losing streak in Texas</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:40320ffb1004aa4ed37e8485129ef15bd95ea4c0a610e9f0dc948c1d0ce850ff</id><updated>2026-11-04T16:25:55Z</updated><published>2026-11-03T16:45:33Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261103-us-midterms-talarico-seeks-to-break-democrats-30-year-losing-streak-in-texas" /><summary type="html">In Texas, Democratic candidate James Talarico is leading the polls ahead of a high-stakes US Senate race against Republican Ken Paxton. The contest is one of the most closely watched battles of the midterm elections, with Democrats hoping to end a 30-year losing streak in the state. FRANCE 24's Andrés Espinal reports from Austin, Texas.</summary></entry><entry><title>Election officials brace for possible Trump interference in the midterms</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:07fc9cb01017611d1e18ae38281668585ca00a07be4bdb4955615021c9a27951</id><updated>2026-11-04T16:25:55Z</updated><published>2026-11-03T16:41:47Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261103-how-state-officials-prepared-for-possible-trump-interference-in-the-midterms" /><summary type="html">State officials who run US elections braced for possible intervention by President Donald Trump's administration in Tuesday's midterms, fearing federal authorities loyal to the president could disrupt voting, challenge ballot counts or try to block the certification of unfavorable results.</summary><category term="Americas" /></entry><entry><title>Brazil keeps a close eye on US midterm elections</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:5a016d07357902e44050e4c93f8cf6e4ba054b9e775022f750f5b3a3b065aa50</id><updated>2026-11-04T16:25:55Z</updated><published>2026-11-03T16:36:07Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261103-brazil-keeps-a-close-eye-on-us-midterm-elections" /><summary type="html">Brazil is keeping a close eye on the US midterm elections. Since Donald Trump's return to the White House, relations between Brasilia and Washington have grown increasingly tense. On the economic front, aggressive trade policies have subjected Brazilian imports to some of the steepest tariffs globally, exacerbating friction with the Trump administration. FRANCE 24's correspondent in Rio de Janeiro Jan Onoszko has more.</summary></entry></feed>
